{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HKBXAHty4sRb9tcJb5Z3da","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Corpus 02 — Quarterly Business Review\n2\t\n3\t**Stresses:** data fidelity · charts and tables · high information density · action titles\n4\t**Target length:** 12–15 slides\n5\t**Audience:** an executive committee reading on laptops in a meeting room; the deck is\n6\tcirculated afterwards and must stand alone\n7\t**Failure modes this corpus exposes:** invented numbers, charts rendered as flat images,\n8\ttables that overflow the slide, decorative styling that fights readability, titles that\n9\tlabel (\"Q3 Revenue\") instead of asserting (\"Q3 revenue grew 12% on enterprise renewals\")\n10\t\n11\t> All content below is fictional. \"Northwind Logistics\" is an invented company used solely\n12\t> as neutral test material. Every figure is fabricated for layout testing.\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Brief\n17\t\n18\tNorthwind Logistics Q3 FY26 business review for the executive committee. Reading-first\n19\tdensity: each slide must be comprehensible without a presenter. Action titles required —\n20\tevery slide title states the finding, not the topic.\n21\t\n22\t**Hard requirement:** every number below must appear in the output exactly as given.\n23\tAny figure in the deck that is not in this document is a hallucination and scores zero\n24\ton content fidelity.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## Content\n29\t\n30\t### Headline\n31\t\n32\tQ3 revenue reached **$47.2M**, up **12.4%** year over year, but gross margin\n33\tcompressed **180 bps** on fuel and contract-labor costs.\n34\t\n35\t### Revenue by segment\n36\t\n37\t| Segment | Q3 FY26 | Q3 FY25 | YoY |\n38\t|---|---:|---:|---:|\n39\t| Freight brokerage | $24.1M | $20.8M | +15.9% |\n40\t| Warehousing | $13.6M | $12.9M | +5.4% |\n41\t| Last-mile delivery | $7.9M | $6.4M | +23.4% |\n42\t| Customs & compliance | $1.6M | $1.9M | −15.8% |\n43\t| **Total** | **$47.2M** | **$42.0M** | **+12.4%** |\n44\t\n45\t### Quarterly revenue trend\n46\t\n47\tEight quarters, $M:\n48\t\n49\t| Quarter | Revenue |\n50\t|---|---:|\n51\t| Q4 FY24 | 36.1 |\n52\t| Q1 FY25 | 37.8 |\n53\t| Q2 FY25 | 39.9 |\n54\t| Q3 FY25 | 42.0 |\n55\t| Q4 FY25 | 41.2 |\n56\t| Q1 FY26 | 43.5 |\n57\t| Q2 FY26 | 45.1 |\n58\t| Q3 FY26 | 47.2 |\n59\t\n60\t### Margin\n61\t\n62\tGross margin fell from **31.2%** to **29.4%**. Two drivers:\n63\t\n64\t- **Fuel** — up 14% per mile against a flat contracted rate card. Impact: **−110 bps**.\n65\t- **Contract labor** — peak-season warehouse staffing 22% above plan. Impact: **−70 bps**.\n66\t\n67\tOperating margin held at **8.1%** because SG&A grew only 3.2% against 12.4% revenue growth.\n68\t\n69\t### Customer concentration is improving\n70\t\n71\tTop-10 customer share fell from **44%** to **38%** year over year — the fourth\n72\tconsecutive quarter of decline. Net new logos: **31**, against **18** in Q3 FY25.\n73\t\n74\tChurn by count was **4.2%**; churn by revenue was **2.1%**, meaning the accounts we\n75\tlost were smaller than average.\n76\t\n77\t### Last-mile is the growth engine but not yet profitable\n78\t\n79\tLast-mile grew 23.4% and now represents 16.7% of revenue, up from 15.2%.\n80\tSegment contribution margin remains **−3.4%**, improving from −7.9% a year ago.\n81\tBreak-even is projected in **Q2 FY27** at current trajectory.\n82\t\n83\t### Headcount\n84\t\n85\t| Function | Q3 FY26 | Change QoQ |\n86\t|---|---:|---:|\n87\t| Operations | 812 | +34 |\n88\t| Engineering | 96 | +11 |\n89\t| Sales & marketing | 143 | +6 |\n90\t| G&A | 71 | −2 |\n91\t| **Total** | **1,122** | **+49** |\n92\t\n93\t### Risks\n94\t\n95\t1. **Fuel exposure is unhedged beyond Q1 FY27.** A further 10% move costs an estimated\n96\t   $1.8M annualized.\n97\t2. **Warehouse lease renewals** in Memphis and Reno land in Q4 FY26, covering 340k sq ft\n98\t   at rates last set in 2021.\n99\t3. **Customs segment decline** is structural, not cyclical — two clients insourced.\n100\t\n101\t### Asks\n102\t\n103\t- Approve **$2.4M** capex for Reno automation, targeting a 15-month payback.\n104\t- Approve fuel hedging through Q4 FY27 at up to 60% of projected volume.\n105\t- Approve **8 incremental engineering roles** to accelerate the last-mile routing rebuild.\n106\t\n107\t### Q4 outlook\n108\t\n109\tRevenue guidance **$48.5M–$50.0M**. Gross margin expected to recover **60–90 bps**\n110\tas peak-season contract labor rolls off.\n111\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a0129d1a-7357-497f-8c10-beefca89c257","uuid":"f128253a-fd93-4fe8-af2a-7cf2f08b17eb","timestamp":"2026-07-28T16:24:06.661Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/bm/2cq1vtmd5wg6k0vdjfpvbmww0000gn/T/ppt-run-ppt-master-rl_h7shm/input.md","content":"# Corpus 02 — Quarterly Business Review\n\n**Stresses:** data fidelity · charts and tables · high information density · action titles\n**Target length:** 12–15 slides\n**Audience:** an executive committee reading on laptops in a meeting room; the deck is\ncirculated afterwards and must stand alone\n**Failure modes this corpus exposes:** invented numbers, charts rendered as flat images,\ntables that overflow the slide, decorative styling that fights readability, titles that\nlabel (\"Q3 Revenue\") instead of asserting (\"Q3 revenue grew 12% on enterprise renewals\")\n\n> All content below is fictional. \"Northwind Logistics\" is an invented company used solely\n> as neutral test material. Every figure is fabricated for layout testing.\n\n---\n\n## Brief\n\nNorthwind Logistics Q3 FY26 business review for the executive committee. Reading-first\ndensity: each slide must be comprehensible without a presenter. Action titles required —\nevery slide title states the finding, not the topic.\n\n**Hard requirement:** every number below must appear in the output exactly as given.\nAny figure in the deck that is not in this document is a hallucination and scores zero\non content fidelity.\n\n---\n\n## Content\n\n### Headline\n\nQ3 revenue reached **$47.2M**, up **12.4%** year over year, but gross margin\ncompressed **180 bps** on fuel and contract-labor costs.\n\n### Revenue by segment\n\n| Segment | Q3 FY26 | Q3 FY25 | YoY |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Freight brokerage | $24.1M | $20.8M | +15.9% |\n| Warehousing | $13.6M | $12.9M | +5.4% |\n| Last-mile delivery | $7.9M | $6.4M | +23.4% |\n| Customs & compliance | $1.6M | $1.9M | −15.8% |\n| **Total** | **$47.2M** | **$42.0M** | **+12.4%** |\n\n### Quarterly revenue trend\n\nEight quarters, $M:\n\n| Quarter | Revenue |\n|---|---:|\n| Q4 FY24 | 36.1 |\n| Q1 FY25 | 37.8 |\n| Q2 FY25 | 39.9 |\n| Q3 FY25 | 42.0 |\n| Q4 FY25 | 41.2 |\n| Q1 FY26 | 43.5 |\n| Q2 FY26 | 45.1 |\n| Q3 FY26 | 47.2 |\n\n### Margin\n\nGross margin fell from **31.2%** to **29.4%**. Two drivers:\n\n- **Fuel** — up 14% per mile against a flat contracted rate card. Impact: **−110 bps**.\n- **Contract labor** — peak-season warehouse staffing 22% above plan. Impact: **−70 bps**.\n\nOperating margin held at **8.1%** because SG&A grew only 3.2% against 12.4% revenue growth.\n\n### Customer concentration is improving\n\nTop-10 customer share fell from **44%** to **38%** year over year — the fourth\nconsecutive quarter of decline. Net new logos: **31**, against **18** in Q3 FY25.\n\nChurn by count was **4.2%**; churn by revenue was **2.1%**, meaning the accounts we\nlost were smaller than average.\n\n### Last-mile is the growth engine but not yet profitable\n\nLast-mile grew 23.4% and now represents 16.7% of revenue, up from 15.2%.\nSegment contribution margin remains **−3.4%**, improving from −7.9% a year ago.\nBreak-even is projected in **Q2 FY27** at current trajectory.\n\n### Headcount\n\n| Function | Q3 FY26 | Change QoQ |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Operations | 812 | +34 |\n| Engineering | 96 | +11 |\n| Sales & marketing | 143 | +6 |\n| G&A | 71 | −2 |\n| **Total** | **1,122** | **+49** |\n\n### Risks\n\n1. **Fuel exposure is unhedged beyond Q1 FY27.** A further 10% move costs an estimated\n   $1.8M annualized.\n2. **Warehouse lease renewals** in Memphis and Reno land in Q4 FY26, covering 340k sq ft\n   at rates last set in 2021.\n3. **Customs segment decline** is structural, not cyclical — two clients insourced.\n\n### Asks\n\n- Approve **$2.4M** capex for Reno automation, targeting a 15-month payback.\n- Approve fuel hedging through Q4 FY27 at up to 60% of projected volume.\n- Approve **8 incremental engineering roles** to accelerate the last-mile routing rebuild.\n\n### Q4 outlook\n\nRevenue guidance **$48.5M–$50.0M**. Gross margin expected to recover **60–90 bps**\nas peak-season contract labor rolls off.\n","numLines":111,"startLine":1,"totalLines":111}}}
